export const DATA_52: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-064", num: 64, title: "Đề 064", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Impact of Light Pollution on Modern Society", paragraphs: ["For most of human history, the night sky was one of the most awe-inspiring sights available to anyone willing to look up. Ancient civilizations used the stars to navigate the seas, mark the changing seasons, and create calendars that guided the planting and harvesting of crops. Artists, scientists, and storytellers drew inspiration from the vast, glittering canopy overhead. Today, however, more than one-third of the world's population can no longer see the Milky Way from where they live. The culprit is light pollution — the excessive and often unnecessary use of artificial light at night — and its effects are far more widespread and serious than most people realize.", "Light pollution occurs when artificial light from cities, roads, buildings, and outdoor advertising brightens the night sky, creating what scientists call a \"skyglow\" over urban and suburban areas. This glow can be seen from dozens of kilometers away and effectively washes out the natural darkness of the night. It has become so pervasive in many parts of the world that children growing up in large cities may never see a truly dark sky or experience the wonder of a star-filled night without traveling far from their homes. The problem is not limited to astronomy. Researchers have found that light pollution affects wildlife, human health, energy consumption, and even the cultural heritage of communities that have long depended on the night sky for their identity and traditions.", "The effects of light pollution on wildlife are particularly well documented. Many animals rely on natural cycles of light and darkness to regulate their behavior, including feeding, migration, and reproduction. Sea turtles, for example, hatch from their eggs at night and instinctively move toward the brightest horizon, which in nature is the ocean reflecting the moonlight. In areas near illuminated beaches, hatchlings become confused and move inland instead, where they die from dehydration or are killed by predators. Migratory birds that navigate by the stars are similarly disrupted, often flying into brightly lit buildings in their thousands. Insects, which are essential pollinators for many of the world's food crops, are drawn to artificial lights in great numbers, where they exhaust themselves circling the source and fail to carry out their vital ecological role.", "Human health has also been affected in ways that scientists are increasingly concerned about. The human body depends on darkness at night to produce melatonin, a hormone that regulates sleep and plays a role in supporting the immune system. Exposure to artificial light at night — even from small sources such as a phone screen or a bedside lamp — can suppress melatonin production and disrupt sleep patterns. Researchers have linked chronic sleep disruption caused by light exposure to a range of health problems, including increased risk of obesity, depression, diabetes, and certain types of cancer. People who work night shifts and are regularly exposed to bright artificial light have shown particularly high rates of these health conditions, prompting medical organizations around the world to call for greater awareness of the risks associated with nighttime light exposure.", "Light pollution also represents a significant waste of energy and financial resources. A large proportion of outdoor lighting — including streetlights, commercial signs, and decorative lighting on buildings — is poorly designed and sends light upward into the sky rather than downward onto the surfaces where it is actually needed. This wasted light serves no practical purpose but consumes enormous amounts of electricity. Studies in several countries have estimated that up to thirty percent of all outdoor lighting is wasted in this way, costing billions of dollars annually and generating unnecessary carbon emissions that contribute to climate change. The good news is that this problem is technically straightforward to address: better-designed light fixtures that direct light downward, combined with timers and motion sensors that ensure lights are only on when needed, can dramatically reduce wasted light without sacrificing safety or visibility.", "Efforts to address light pollution have grown steadily in recent years. A number of communities around the world have established what are known as \"dark sky preserves\" — protected areas where artificial light is carefully controlled so that residents and visitors can experience genuinely dark skies. Organizations such as the International Dark-Sky Association work with governments, businesses, and communities to promote responsible outdoor lighting practices and certify areas that meet their standards. Schools and astronomy clubs have also played an important role in raising public awareness, encouraging people to turn off unnecessary lights, use warm-colored bulbs that are less disruptive to wildlife, and appreciate the natural beauty of the night sky.", "Light pollution is a problem that is rarely treated with the same urgency as other forms of environmental damage, yet its effects on human health, wildlife, energy use, and cultural heritage are real and growing. The encouraging reality is that, unlike many environmental challenges, it is one that can be significantly reduced with relatively simple and affordable changes to the way we design and use artificial lighting. Choosing to take the night sky seriously — not just as an aesthetic pleasure but as a shared natural resource worth protecting — is a step that individuals, communities, and governments can all take, with benefits that would be felt by both people and the wider natural world."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Light pollution has serious and wide-ranging effects on wildlife, human health, energy use, and culture, but can be meaningfully reduced through better lighting practices.", "Light pollution is mainly an astronomical problem because it prevents people from seeing stars and the Milky Way.", "Governments around the world have already taken effective steps to reduce light pollution and protect dark skies.", "The most urgent problem caused by light pollution is the damage it causes to sea turtles and migratory birds."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"It\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the distance from which city lights can be seen at night", "the brightness of outdoor advertising in large cities", "the natural darkness of the night sky", "the skyglow created by artificial light over urban and suburban areas"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do sea turtle hatchlings sometimes move inland instead of toward the ocean?", options: ["Rising sea levels have changed the shape of beaches, making it harder for hatchlings to find the water.", "They are attracted to the warmth produced by roads and buildings near the beach.", "Predators on the beach force them to change direction and seek shelter on land.", "Artificial lights on and near beaches confuse them, causing them to move toward the wrong direction."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does artificial light at night affect the human body?", options: ["It directly damages cells in the eyes and skin through prolonged exposure to blue-spectrum light.", "It increases the production of melatonin, causing people to feel sleepy during daylight hours.", "It raises body temperature at night, which interferes with the body's natural cooling process during sleep.", "It can suppress melatonin production and disrupt sleep patterns, which has been linked to various health problems."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how much outdoor lighting is estimated to be wasted in some countries?", options: ["Up to thirty percent", "Up to ten percent", "Nearly half of all outdoor lighting", "Around twenty percent"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"hatch\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gather in large groups on the beach", "emerge from an egg", "dig a hole in the sand to find shelter", "swim rapidly toward the nearest source of light"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do \"dark sky preserves\" aim to achieve?", options: ["They provide areas where scientists can conduct astronomical research without interference from city lights.", "They are protected zones where artificial light is carefully controlled so that people can experience genuinely dark skies.", "They are sections of cities where all outdoor advertising and commercial lighting must be switched off after midnight.", "They are nature reserves where animals that depend on darkness for survival are protected from all human activity."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a health problem linked to chronic exposure to artificial light at night?", options: ["Obesity", "High blood pressure", "Certain types of cancer", "Depression"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"pervasive\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["spreading widely and present in many places", "growing more intense every year", "difficult to see without special equipment", "easy to measure and track scientifically"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the author's view of light pollution as an environmental problem?", options: ["The author argues that governments are solely responsible for solving light pollution and that individuals can do very little on their own.", "The author implies that light pollution, unlike many environmental problems, is solvable through relatively accessible changes and deserves greater attention.", "The author believes light pollution is impossible to solve without major changes to the global energy system.", "The author thinks people will only take light pollution seriously once it causes more obvious damage to human health."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Super Monster Wolf: Japan's Robotic Solution to Wildlife Conflicts", paragraphs: ["In the mountainous rural regions of Japan, farmers have long waged an unequal battle against wildlife. Wild boars, deer, and monkeys descend from the forests surrounding agricultural communities to feast on crops, destroying months of careful cultivation in a single night. Traditional deterrents — scarecrows, fences, traps, and even firecrackers — have proven increasingly ineffective as animals grow accustomed to them and learn to ignore their warnings. In recent years, however, a distinctly twenty-first century solution has emerged from a collaboration between agricultural technology developers and the pressing needs of Japan's rural farming communities: a solar-powered robotic device known as the Super Monster Wolf.", "The Super Monster Wolf is a wolf-shaped mechanical device approximately sixty-five centimeters long and weighing around five kilograms. Its exterior is covered in realistic-looking fur, and its eyes are fitted with flashing red LED lights designed to mimic the glowing gaze of a predator at night. When the device detects movement within a range of roughly one kilometer using an infrared motion sensor, it activates automatically, emitting one of more than fifty pre-recorded sounds including wolf howls, human voices, gunshots, and other threatening noises. The sounds are broadcast at high volume through a speaker built into the device's body and rotate between recordings to prevent animals from becoming habituated to any single sound. A solar panel on the device's back provides its power supply, making it entirely self-sufficient and suitable for deployment in remote locations without access to electrical infrastructure.", "The device was developed by a Japanese agricultural machinery company in response to the growing severity of wildlife crop damage in rural Japan. The country has seen a dramatic increase in wildlife populations in recent decades, partly as a result of the decline of traditional hunting culture and the aging and depopulation of rural communities, which has reduced both the human presence that once kept animals at bay and the number of people available to manage wildlife actively. In some prefectures, the annual cost of crop damage caused by wild animals has run into tens of millions of yen, threatening the economic viability of farming communities that are already struggling with falling incomes, aging populations, and the migration of younger generations to urban centers.", "Initial field trials of the Super Monster Wolf were conducted in rice paddies and vegetable fields in Hokkaido, Japan's northernmost main island, an area particularly affected by wild boar incursions. The results were striking : farmers who deployed the device reported near-total elimination of wild boar activity in the protected fields over the trial period. Wildlife monitoring confirmed that boars that had previously visited these fields on a nightly basis had entirely avoided the areas where the devices were placed, with their movements tracked moving away from farmland toward less protected areas of forest. Based on these encouraging outcomes, the manufacturer began offering the device for commercial sale and has since received orders from farming communities across Japan as well as from agricultural operators in other countries facing similar wildlife challenges.", "The design logic of the Super Monster Wolf draws on established principles from the science of animal behavior and wildlife management. Predator cue technology — the use of sights, sounds, or scents associated with natural predators to deter prey animals — has a long history in conservation and agricultural settings. What distinguishes the Super Monster Wolf from earlier applications of this principle is the combination of visual realism, sound variety, and automatic activation that makes it significantly more difficult for target animals to habituate to the device over time. Research in animal cognition has consistently shown that habituation is the primary mechanism by which traditional deterrents fail: once an animal has confirmed through repeated experience that a scarecrow or a fixed alarm poses no genuine threat, it will cease to respond. The rotating sound library and unpredictable activation pattern of the Super Monster Wolf are specifically engineered to delay this process.", "Despite the enthusiasm with which the device has been received in some quarters, researchers and wildlife managers have raised a number of important questions about its long-term effectiveness and broader implications. Chief among these is the question of habituation over extended deployment periods — whether the variety of sounds and visual stimuli built into the device will prove sufficient to prevent target animals from eventually learning that it poses no real danger. Some wildlife researchers have also expressed concern about the potential for the device to displace rather than genuinely deter animals, pushing wildlife into neighboring unprotected areas and potentially intensifying human-wildlife conflict in those locations rather than reducing it overall. The use of wolf imagery raises additional considerations in a country where wolves have been extinct for over a century, prompting questions about what role, if any, the symbolic reintroduction of predator imagery plays in shaping long-term animal behavior at the landscape scale.", "The Super Monster Wolf represents a creative and technologically sophisticated response to a challenge that is becoming increasingly urgent not only in Japan but across many parts of the world where expanding wildlife populations and encroaching agricultural land are bringing animals and farmers into ever-closer and more contentious contact. Whether it will prove to be a durable solution or a temporary fix will depend on how animals respond over time and whether the device can be refined to stay ahead of the behavioral adaptations that wildlife invariably develop in response to repeated stimuli. What is already clear is that the search for effective, humane, and sustainable approaches to wildlife conflict management is one that will require ongoing innovation — and perhaps a willingness to look beyond conventional boundaries for inspiration."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To warn that robotic wildlife deterrents may cause greater harm to animal populations than the crop damage they are designed to prevent.", "To argue that Japan's Super Monster Wolf is the definitive solution to human-wildlife conflict in agricultural settings worldwide.", "To compare the Super Monster Wolf with traditional deterrent methods and demonstrate why robotic solutions are always superior.", "To describe the design and field performance of the Super Monster Wolf while also examining the questions its long-term effectiveness raises."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"striking\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["difficult to interpret without more data", "disappointing and below expectations", "consistent with what researchers had predicted", "impressive and immediately noticeable"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes the physical design of the Super Monster Wolf?", options: ["A lightweight drone that patrols farmland at night and mimics the sounds of several different predators.", "A large mechanical device shaped like a bear, equipped with cameras and a GPS tracking system.", "A wolf-shaped robot approximately sixty-five centimeters long with flashing red LED eyes and a speaker that emits rotating sounds.", "A motion-activated sprinkler system disguised as a wolf and powered by a battery that must be recharged weekly."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what factors have contributed to the increase in wildlife crop damage in rural Japan?", options: ["The expansion of protected forest reserves that has pushed animals closer to farmland", "The reduction of government subsidies for wildlife management programs in rural prefectures", "The introduction of new crop varieties that are more attractive to wild animals than traditional ones", "The decline of traditional hunting culture and the aging and depopulation of rural communities"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did field trials of the Super Monster Wolf in Hokkaido reveal?", options: ["The device was more effective against deer and monkeys than against wild boars in rice paddy settings.", "Wild boars avoided fields where the device was placed and moved toward less protected areas of forest.", "Animals quickly adapted to the device within two weeks, limiting its effectiveness to short-term deployment.", "The device was only partially effective, reducing wild boar activity by approximately half during the trial period."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the Super Monster Wolf device itself", "the infrared motion sensor built into the device", "the movement detected within the device's sensor range", "the range of approximately one kilometer"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is \"habituation\" and why is it the primary reason traditional deterrents fail?", options: ["Habituation refers to animals developing a physical tolerance to stimuli over time, which means sounds must be played at increasing volumes to remain effective.", "Habituation is the process by which animals learn through repeated experience that a perceived threat poses no genuine danger, causing them to stop responding to it.", "Habituation is a seasonal pattern in animal behavior that makes deterrents effective in some months but completely ineffective in others.", "Habituation is the tendency of animals to return to familiar feeding grounds; it fails deterrents because animals have strong memories of food sources."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"displace\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["force to move from one location to another", "confuse and disorient through unfamiliar stimuli", "permanently eliminate from a given territory", "reduce in population through deterrence over time"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following concerns about the Super Monster Wolf is NOT mentioned?", options: ["The high cost of the device may make it unaffordable for small-scale farmers in rural communities.", "Using wolf imagery raises questions about the role of predator symbolism in a country where wolves are extinct.", "The device may push wildlife into neighboring unprotected areas rather than genuinely reducing conflict.", "Animals may eventually habituate to the device even with its rotating sounds and visual stimuli."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the author's view of the Super Monster Wolf as a long-term solution?", options: ["The author is confident the Super Monster Wolf will remain effective indefinitely because of its sophisticated rotating sound system.", "The author implies that the device is a promising but uncertain solution, and that durable wildlife conflict management will require continued innovation.", "The author believes the device should be banned because displacing wildlife causes more harm than the crop damage it prevents.", "The author suggests that Japan should focus on reintroducing real wolves rather than relying on robotic imitations."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Rise of Job Hopping in the Modern Labor Market", paragraphs: ["For much of the twentieth century, the dominant model of professional success in most developed economies was defined by loyalty and longevity. An employee who joined a company in their twenties and retired from it four decades later was not regarded as someone who had lacked ambition, but as someone who had demonstrated the kind of commitment and reliability that employers valued above almost all other qualities. Careers were conceived as vertical structures — a ladder to be climbed steadily within a single organization — and departures before retirement were often viewed with suspicion by subsequent employers, who interpreted them as signs of instability or poor performance. This model, sometimes called the \"organizational career,\" shaped recruitment practices, benefit structures, and workplace cultures across entire industries for generations.", "That model has changed dramatically. The practice of changing jobs every two to three years — widely termed \"job hopping\" — has shifted from a red flag in recruitment contexts to an increasingly normalized, and in some industries actively celebrated, career strategy. [1] Research conducted by human resources analytics firms has documented a consistent decline over the past three decades in median employee tenure across developed economies, with the steepest drops concentrated in technology, creative industries, and professional services. Younger workers, in particular, have embraced more fluid career trajectories, moving between employers, industries, and even countries in pursuit of higher compensation, accelerated skill development, and working environments that align with their personal values. The question of whether this shift represents a genuine improvement in how labor markets function — or a fragmentation of professional identity with significant costs — remains actively contested.", "The economic case for job hopping rests on a body of evidence that is difficult to dismiss. Study after study has found that, in most sectors, the single most effective mechanism for increasing salary is not internal promotion but external movement: employees who change jobs typically receive pay increases of ten to twenty percent on transition, compared with average annual raises of two to three percent for those who remain with their current employer. [2] Over a career spanning several decades, this compounding differential can amount to hundreds of thousands of dollars in foregone earnings for workers who prioritize loyalty over mobility. Beyond compensation, frequent job changers often accumulate a broader and more diverse skill set than their counterparts who remain within a single organizational context, making them more adaptable and, in theory, more resilient to the disruptions — automation, industry consolidation, economic downturns — that characterize contemporary labor markets.", "Employers and organizational researchers, however, have raised a countervailing set of concerns. The costs associated with employee turnover are substantial and frequently underestimated: recruiting, onboarding, and training a replacement employee typically costs between fifty and two hundred percent of that employee's annual salary, depending on the seniority and specialization of the role. Beyond direct financial costs, high turnover rates erode institutional knowledge — the accumulated, often tacit understanding of organizational processes, client relationships, and product histories that cannot be codified in any training manual and is lost each time an experienced employee departs. [3] Industries with particularly long knowledge accumulation cycles, such as aerospace engineering, pharmaceutical development, and high-stakes financial risk management, have reported that chronic turnover creates systemic vulnerabilities that periodic recruitment cannot fully repair.", "The relationship between job hopping and personal wellbeing is more nuanced than either its advocates or critics typically acknowledge. Workers who change jobs frequently report higher short-term satisfaction — novelty, increased compensation, and a sense of agency over their professional trajectory are all reliably associated with elevated wellbeing scores in surveys. Yet longitudinal research tracking the same individuals across decades has identified a more complicated picture: those who change employers very frequently, particularly in a pattern driven primarily by escape from dissatisfaction rather than pursuit of genuine opportunity, show higher rates of professional disillusionment by mid-career than those who changed at a more measured pace. The distinction researchers draw is between strategic mobility — deliberate movement toward specific goals — and reactive mobility — movement driven by avoidance — with the former associated with better long-term outcomes across multiple dimensions.", "The structural conditions that have enabled and encouraged job hopping are not merely cultural but deeply institutional. The declining prevalence of defined-benefit pension schemes — which historically tied retirement security to tenure with a single employer — has removed one of the most powerful financial incentives for remaining with any given organization. The rise of portable skills credentials, professional certification programs, and LinkedIn profiles as primary repositories of professional identity has shifted the organizational unit of professional life from the firm to the individual. Remote work, whose normalization was dramatically accelerated by the COVID-19 pandemic, has expanded the effective labor market available to any individual worker to a potentially global scale, further reducing the geographic and logistical barriers to changing employers. [4]", "What the evolution of job hopping ultimately reveals is not simply a change in individual career preferences but a fundamental restructuring of the implicit contract between employers and employees. The postwar expectation of mutual long-term commitment — in which employers offered security and employees offered loyalty — has been progressively replaced by a transactional relationship in which both parties implicitly acknowledge that the arrangement will last only as long as it serves each party's interests. Whether this reconfiguration represents an honest recognition of economic reality or a deterioration of the social fabric that once bound workplaces into genuine communities remains, like so many questions about the future of work, genuinely open."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["The passage examines the economic, organizational, and personal dimensions of job hopping, presenting it as a contested phenomenon that reflects broader changes in the employment relationship.", "The passage argues that job hopping is universally beneficial and should be encouraged by employers as a recruitment strategy.", "The passage focuses primarily on the financial advantages that younger workers gain through frequent job changes compared to those who stay with one employer.", "The passage warns that high rates of job hopping are eroding institutional knowledge to a degree that threatens entire industries."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do salary increases from changing jobs typically compare to annual raises for employees who remain with their current employer?", options: ["Job changers receive slightly lower immediate pay increases but recover the difference through faster promotion once in their new roles.", "Job changers typically receive increases of ten to twenty percent, compared with average annual raises of two to three percent for those who stay.", "Job changers typically receive increases of five to ten percent, compared with annual raises of four to five percent for long-tenured employees.", "Both groups typically receive similar salary increases of around five percent, but job changers benefit more from non-financial perks."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on information from both paragraphs 2 and 3, which of the following best explains why job hopping has become increasingly accepted in modern labor markets?", options: ["Employers have lowered their standards for loyalty because of widespread labor shortages across most industries.", "Government policies in most developed countries now require employers to offer equal consideration to candidates with diverse employment histories.", "Documented evidence that mobility produces superior financial outcomes and broader skill development has given the practice economic legitimacy that was previously lacking.", "Younger workers have collectively decided to prioritize personal values over financial compensation when making career decisions."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one significant concern employers have about high employee turnover?", options: ["Frequent turnover forces companies to raise salaries beyond sustainable levels in order to attract replacement candidates.", "High turnover rates reduce consumer confidence in companies and damage brand reputation in competitive markets.", "Employers struggle to maintain consistent product quality when key employees leave before completing long-term projects.", "The loss of tacit institutional knowledge that departing employees carry with them cannot be fully recovered through recruitment."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did longitudinal research reveal about workers who change employers very frequently?", options: ["They report lower short-term satisfaction than workers who stay in the same organization but recover their sense of wellbeing within a year of each transition.", "They tend to show higher rates of professional disillusionment by mid-career, particularly when changes are driven primarily by avoidance rather than opportunity.", "They consistently earn more over a full career than workers who change jobs at a more moderate pace.", "They develop stronger professional networks than their more stable counterparts, which provides resilience during economic downturns."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a structural factor that has enabled job hopping in the modern labor market?", options: ["The rise of portable skills credentials and professional certification programs", "The normalization of remote work expanding the available labor market for individual workers", "Generational shifts in attitudes toward authority and organizational hierarchy", "The declining prevalence of defined-benefit pension schemes tied to tenure"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What conclusion can be drawn by combining information from paragraphs 4 and 5 about when job hopping is most likely to produce negative outcomes?", options: ["Job hopping produces the most negative outcomes when it occurs in industries that rely heavily on institutional knowledge and when it is driven by avoidance of dissatisfaction rather than deliberate pursuit of opportunity.", "Job hopping is most damaging when it occurs during economic downturns, when the number of available positions is insufficient to absorb a mobile workforce.", "Job hopping is most harmful when it occurs in the early stages of a career, before workers have accumulated the skills needed to negotiate competitive salaries.", "Job hopping produces the worst outcomes in large organizations where replacement costs are highest and where tacit knowledge is most difficult to document."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["employees who change jobs frequently and accumulate diverse skill sets", "employers who offer above-average annual pay raises to retain high-performing staff", "organizational researchers who study the long-term effects of career mobility", "industries characterized by high levels of automation and workforce disruption"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"strategic\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["based on advice from professional career coaches and recruitment specialists", "motivated primarily by financial gain rather than personal development", "carried out with clear purpose and deliberate planning toward specific goals", "undertaken impulsively without considering long-term professional consequences"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"In the technology sector, for instance, a candidate whose resume shows five employers over ten years is increasingly read as a marker of broad exposure and adaptability rather than unreliability.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Sophisticated Social Structures of Ant Colonies", paragraphs: ["Among the most studied and yet persistently surprising organisms in the biological sciences, ants occupy a position of remarkable distinction. With over twenty thousand known species distributed across nearly every terrestrial habitat on Earth, the ant family Formicidae represents one of evolution's most spectacularly successful experiments in social living. Individually, an ant is a creature of modest cognitive capacity — its brain contains roughly 250,000 neurons, compared with the 86 billion found in the human brain. Yet the colonies these insects form are capable of collective behaviors of extraordinary sophistication: constructing architecturally complex underground cities, waging coordinated military campaigns, farming fungi, herding aphids, and solving spatial optimization problems that continue to inspire algorithms in computer science and logistics. [1] The source of this apparent paradox — extreme individual simplicity giving rise to remarkable collective intelligence — lies at the heart of what makes ant biology one of the most intellectually generative fields in contemporary science.", "The organizational architecture of an ant colony is built around a division of labor so precise and so effectively maintained that it has invited sustained comparison with the functioning of human economies and institutions. At the apex of this hierarchy stands the queen — or, in many species, multiple queens — whose primary biological function is reproduction. A mature queen of the leafcutter ant species Atta sexdens may lay up to thirty thousand eggs per day and live for fifteen years or more, serving as the colony's sole reproductive engine throughout that span. [2] Below the reproductive caste, the colony is populated by workers — sterile females who carry out virtually all of the colony's non-reproductive functions, including foraging, nest construction and maintenance, brood care, and defense. In many species, workers are further differentiated into physical subcastes — minor workers, media workers, and soldiers — whose body size and morphology are calibrated to the specific demands of their functional roles.", "The mechanisms through which labor is allocated and coordinated within an ant colony have been the subject of intense scientific scrutiny since the mid-twentieth century. Early models proposed that allocation was governed by a fixed caste system in which each worker was permanently assigned to a role at the time of its development. More nuanced research has since revealed that while physical caste largely determines the range of tasks a worker is capable of performing, actual task allocation within that range is highly dynamic and responds continuously to the colony's changing needs. When a colony's foraging force suffers losses — due to predation, weather, or depletion of food sources — workers that had previously specialized in nest maintenance may transition to foraging roles with a rapidity that fixed-caste models cannot account for. This behavioral plasticity, coordinated without any central directing intelligence, is achieved through a distributed system of local interactions: chemical signals, tactile exchanges, and the use of pheromone trails that function as a form of collective memory encoding the colony's recent history of resource discovery.", "The phenomenon of self-organization — the emergence of complex, adaptive global patterns from simple, locally governed interactions — is nowhere more vividly illustrated than in the construction behavior of army ants of the genus Eciton. These species build their nests not from fixed materials but from their own bodies: workers interlock their legs and mandibles to form living bridges, ladders, and walls that adapt their architecture in real time to the traffic demands of the colony. When scouts discover a gap in a foraging route, workers arriving at the gap spontaneously assemble into a bridge, widening or narrowing their formation according to the volume of traffic passing over them. No individual ant directs this process; the bridge is assembled and disassembled through purely local responses to contact and chemical cues. [3] The computational elegance of this system has been formally modeled by robotics researchers seeking to design autonomous multi-agent systems capable of analogous collective problem-solving without centralized control.", "The agricultural practices of certain ant lineages represent perhaps the most dramatic illustration of behavioral complexity in the order. Leafcutter ants of the tribe Attini have cultivated fungal gardens for an estimated fifty million years — a continuous agricultural tradition that predates human farming by a margin that renders the latter almost geologically insignificant. Workers harvest leaf fragments not as food directly but as a substrate for the cultivation of a specific fungal species that has co-evolved with the ants over tens of millions of years and exists nowhere in nature outside of ant gardens. The colony's fungal monoculture is vulnerable to parasitic infections, and the ants deploy a sophisticated chemical defense system: workers carry symbiotic bacteria on their cuticles that produce antibiotics targeting the primary fungal pathogen, effectively operating a pharmaceutical factory on their own bodies. [4]", "The cognitive architecture underlying these collective achievements has been a source of intense philosophical as well as scientific interest. The individual ant, operating without awareness of the colony's overall state or objectives, contributes to outcomes of collective intelligence that no individual could plan or comprehend. This dynamic has been described by some biologists as a form of \"superorganism\" — a concept that frames the colony rather than the individual as the primary unit of biological organization and adaptive behavior. Critics of the superorganism framework argue that it risks anthropomorphizing collective insect behavior and obscuring the mechanistic simplicity of the local interactions from which complex outcomes emerge. What is not in dispute is that the colony-level behaviors documented in Formicidae are among the most compelling natural demonstrations of how complexity, adaptability, and apparent purpose can arise from systems operating without any of the deliberate planning that human observers instinctively attribute to them.", "The scientific study of ant colonies has generated insights that extend well beyond entomology, informing research programs in computer science, economics, urban planning, and distributed robotics. Ant colony optimization algorithms — mathematical procedures inspired by the pheromone-trail dynamics of foraging ants — have been applied to problems ranging from the routing of telecommunications networks to the scheduling of airline fleets. The principles of stigmergy — indirect coordination through environmental modification — that govern nest construction and trail formation in ants have been incorporated into the design of autonomous robotic systems intended to operate in unstructured environments without centralized command. In this sense, the ant colony functions not merely as a subject of biological curiosity but as a conceptual laboratory for understanding how intelligence, order, and adaptive complexity can emerge in the absence of a directing mind."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the central argument of the passage?", options: ["The collective behaviors of ant colonies, emerging from the local interactions of cognitively simple individuals, represent a form of distributed intelligence with wide scientific implications.", "Ant colonies are the most evolutionarily successful organisms on Earth and offer a model that human societies should actively seek to replicate.", "Recent advances in robotics and computer science have finally allowed researchers to fully decode the mechanisms governing ant colony organization.", "The superorganism framework is the most accurate and useful conceptual model for understanding collective behavior in social insects."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"transition\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually reduce activity levels as a colony approaches its maximum population", "shift from one function or state to another in response to changing circumstances", "permanently abandon a role after sustained exposure to adverse conditions", "compete with other workers for access to preferred tasks within the colony"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do army ants of the genus Eciton construct bridges along their foraging routes?", options: ["Workers interlock their own bodies to form living structures that adapt in real time to the volume of traffic passing over them.", "They exploit existing features of the landscape, such as fallen branches and root systems, anchoring them in place using mandible grip.", "They secrete a chemical adhesive that bonds soil particles and plant matter into a rigid structure spanning gaps in the terrain.", "They assign a specialized subcaste of builders to identify gaps in advance and prepare crossing points before the main foraging column arrives."], answer: 0 }, { q: "What does the author imply in paragraphs 3 and 4 about the nature of coordination within ant colonies?", options: ["The apparent sophistication of ant colony coordination is largely illusory and can be fully explained by the genetically programmed instincts of individual workers.", "Coordination in ant colonies is ultimately directed by the queen, who communicates task assignments through pheromone signals distributed throughout the nest.", "Coordination becomes effective only when colonies reach a critical population size at which pheromone networks achieve sufficient density to transmit information reliably.", "Complex and adaptive colony-level coordination emerges from purely local interactions between individuals without any centralized directing intelligence."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a field in which insights from ant colony research have been applied?", options: ["Routing of telecommunications networks", "Design of medical diagnostic systems", "Design of autonomous robotic systems", "Scheduling of airline fleets"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"deploy\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["share with neighboring colonies through interspecies chemical signaling", "temporarily suppress during periods of low fungal infection risk", "systematically bring into use for a specific protective purpose", "accidentally develop through a process of random genetic mutation"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What does the author imply about leafcutter ant agriculture in paragraph 5?", options: ["Leafcutter ant agriculture is primarily significant because it demonstrates that tool use is not unique to vertebrate animals.", "The fifty-million-year continuity of leafcutter ant fungal cultivation illustrates a level of behavioral and biological integration that surpasses even the most complex human agricultural systems in longevity and co-evolutionary sophistication.", "Leafcutter ant agriculture is fundamentally similar to human farming and should be understood primarily as an early precursor to the agricultural revolution.", "The vulnerability of leafcutter ant fungal monocultures to parasitic infection demonstrates that their agricultural system, despite its age, is ultimately less stable than diversified farming strategies."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What tension does the author identify in paragraph 6 regarding the \"superorganism\" concept?", options: ["The tension between biologists who favor the superorganism framework and ecologists who argue that colony boundaries are too fluid for the concept to be meaningfully applied.", "The difficulty of applying a framework developed for insect societies to the study of collective behavior in vertebrate animals such as wolves and dolphins.", "The disagreement between evolutionary biologists and cognitive scientists over whether the colony or the individual queen constitutes the primary unit of adaptive selection.", "The conflict between the explanatory power the superorganism framework offers and the risk that it obscures the mechanistic simplicity of the local interactions from which collective outcomes emerge."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following best characterizes the author's attitude toward the scientific study of ant colonies?", options: ["Narrowly technical, presenting ant biology primarily as a source of engineering solutions rather than as a subject of intrinsic scientific interest.", "Broadly admiring and intellectually engaged, treating ant colony behavior as a genuinely illuminating window into how complex adaptive systems function across multiple scientific domains.", "Philosophically ambivalent, suggesting that the study of ant colonies raises more questions about collective intelligence than it is currently capable of answering.", "Cautiously skeptical, as the author believes the superorganism framework has led researchers to overstate the cognitive complexity of ant behavior."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to the passage? \"The scale of this reproductive output means that the queen functions less as a decision-maker than as a highly specialized biological engine whose contribution to colony life is defined entirely by the rate and quality of her egg production.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-138", num: 138, title: "Đề 138", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Leaving the Nest Early: Britain's Changing Youth Housing Trend", paragraphs: ["For many decades, young people in Britain typically left their family home in their late teens or early twenties, often when they started university or found their first job. However, in recent years, a new pattern has appeared. More young Britons are choosing to move out even earlier, sometimes before they turn eighteen. This shift is changing family life across the country and has become the subject of many discussions among parents, teachers, and researchers alike.", "Several reasons explain this early departure. First, many teenagers feel a strong desire for independence and want to make their own decisions about work, friends, and daily routines. Second, the growth of remote and flexible jobs means that young people no longer need to live near their family home to find work; they can rent a room in a different city and still keep a steady income. Third, some young people leave because they want a change of environment, especially if they live in a small town with few opportunities. Finally, close friendships and shared housing with peers make it easier and more affordable to move out earlier than it used to be.", "Despite these motivations, moving out early is not simple. Renting a flat or a room in Britain has become expensive, especially in big cities like London, Manchester, and Bristol. Many young renters must share a house with several strangers just to afford the rent. Bills for electricity, heating, and the internet add further pressure to a tight budget. Some young people take on two jobs, or extra shifts, just to keep up with monthly payments. Even so, most say they prefer this financial struggle to staying at home longer than they wish.", "This early independence changes family relationships as well. Parents often worry about their children's safety, health, and money problems after they leave. At the same time, many parents notice that their relationship with their child actually improves once the daily tension of living together disappears. Weekly phone calls and occasional weekend visits replace the arguments that used to happen over small chores or curfews. Grandparents, however, sometimes feel that this trend removes a generation of hands-on family support, since young adults are no longer nearby to help with household tasks or care for elderly relatives.", "National surveys show that this trend is growing steadily. According to recent housing data, the average age at which young Britons leave home has dropped compared to the previous decade, even though average rent prices have risen sharply during the same period. Some local councils have started special housing schemes, offering shared apartments at reduced rent for people under the age of twenty-five. Charities also run free workshops that teach practical skills, such as budgeting and cooking, to help them manage their new independent life.", "Whether this trend continues will depend on economic conditions ahead, including rent prices and job opportunities across the country. For now, many young Britons view early independence as a valuable, even necessary, step toward adulthood, regardless of the financial and emotional challenges involved. Their choice reflects a broader shift in how modern British families view growing up and leaving home."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Government housing policies are the main reason more young Britons live independently today.", "Financial difficulties are the primary reason young Britons decide to leave home early, despite a persistent desire to stay close to family.", "A growing number of young people in Britain are leaving home earlier than before, driven mainly by a desire for independence, despite facing financial and social challenges.", "Young Britons who leave home before finishing school rarely maintain contact with their parents."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how can young people move away from home even without living close to a workplace?", options: ["Family businesses provide free housing nearby.", "University tuition is free for those who move out.", "They can find remote or flexible jobs.", "Local councils provide free transport to any city."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do many young renters do to cover their monthly rent?", options: ["Ask their parents for extra money.", "Apply for special government loans.", "Move back home temporarily.", "Take on extra work or share housing with others."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do many parents feel after their children move out?", options: ["They immediately stop contact with their child.", "They feel no change in the relationship at all.", "They insist that their child return home.", "They worry at first but often find the relationship improves."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to recent housing data mentioned in the passage, what has happened to the average age at which young Britons leave home?", options: ["It has only changed in London.", "It has dropped compared to the previous decade.", "It has stayed the same over the past decade.", "It has increased significantly."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a benefit of moving out early?", options: ["Guaranteed access to high-paying jobs.", "Improved relationship with parents.", "Access to subsidised housing schemes.", "Greater personal independence."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"steady\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["affordable", "growing", "occasional", "consistent"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"tension\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["stress", "pressure", "excitement", "budget"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["workshops", "young people under twenty-five", "charities", "local councils"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What can be inferred about grandparents' attitude toward this trend, based on the passage?", options: ["Grandparents insist that grandchildren must return home immediately.", "Grandparents feel this trend removes a generation of hands-on support, but ultimately blame parents for allowing it.", "Grandparents believe this trend will save the family money in the long run.", "Grandparents may feel this trend leaves fewer family members nearby to help with everyday household tasks and elderly care."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Humble Potato: A Global Staple Facing New Challenges", paragraphs: ["Few crops have shaped global food systems as profoundly as the potato. Originating in the Andean highlands of South America several thousand years ago, this humble tuber was cultivated by indigenous communities long before European explorers encountered it in the sixteenth century. Its introduction to Europe initially met with suspicion, as many believed the plant, related to poisonous nightshades, might itself be harmful. Within two centuries, however, the potato had become a dietary staple across much of the continent, a transformation that historians attribute to its remarkable adaptability and calorie density.", "Unlike many staple grains, the potato tends to thrive in a wide range of climates, from temperate lowlands to high-altitude mountain plateaus. Its tolerance of cooler temperatures and relatively poor soils allowed farmers in regions unsuitable for wheat or rice cultivation to achieve reliable yields. This adaptability, combined with a comparatively short growing season, may explain why the crop spread so rapidly once its nutritional value became widely recognised. Modern cultivation techniques, including crop rotation and selective breeding, have further improved consistency in yield across diverse environments.", "Nutritionally, the potato is often misunderstood. Although frequently associated with weight gain, largely due to preparation methods such as frying, the tuber itself is a relatively low-calorie source of complex carbohydrates, potassium, and vitamin C. When consumed with its skin intact, it also provides a meaningful amount of dietary fibre. Nutritionists have increasingly emphasised that it is not the potato itself but the manner of preparation that determines its impact on health, a distinction that public health campaigns have only recently begun to communicate effectively.", "Economically, the potato ranks among the world's most significant food crops, trailing only maize, wheat, and rice in total global production. Millions of smallholder farmers, particularly across Asia, Africa, and Latin America, depend on the crop for both subsistence and income generation. Its short cultivation cycle allows farmers to harvest multiple times within a single year in favourable climates, a factor that has made it an increasingly attractive option for food security initiatives in regions vulnerable to seasonal shortages.", "Despite these advantages, potato cultivation is not without risk. Late blight, a fungal-like disease, devastated European potato harvests during the nineteenth century and remains a persistent threat to farmers worldwide, capable of destroying entire fields within days under humid conditions. Pests such as the Colorado potato beetle further complicate cultivation, often requiring costly intervention. More recently, shifting rainfall patterns and rising temperatures associated with climate change have begun to affect yields in traditionally reliable growing regions, prompting concern among agricultural researchers about long-term food security.", "In response to these pressures , plant scientists have accelerated efforts to develop disease-resistant and drought-tolerant potato varieties through both traditional breeding and genetic engineering. Some researchers argue that such innovations, combined with emerging techniques like vertical and hydroponic farming, could substantially reduce the crop's vulnerability to environmental disruption. Others caution that widespread adoption of these methods may be slowed by regulatory hurdles and public scepticism toward genetically modified crops, particularly in regions with strict agricultural policies.", "Whether these innovations will be sufficient to secure the potato's role in future food systems remains uncertain. What does seem clear, however, is that the crop's remarkable adaptability, both agricultural and culinary, is likely to ensure its continued relevance, even as farmers and scientists navigate the mounting challenges posed by disease, pests, and a changing climate."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The potato's vulnerability to disease is arguably its most defining characteristic, overshadowing its nutritional and economic benefits.", "Potatoes are primarily valuable because they can be prepared in many different culinary styles.", "Genetic engineering has already solved most of the challenges associated with potato cultivation.", "Despite facing significant agricultural and environmental challenges, the potato remains a highly adaptable and economically vital crop whose future importance depends on ongoing scientific innovation."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did potatoes initially face resistance when introduced to Europe?", options: ["They were considered too difficult to cultivate in European soil.", "They were too expensive for most European farmers to purchase.", "They were believed to be related to poisonous nightshade plants.", "They required a much longer growing season than European grains."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, potato production ranks after which crops in total global production?", options: ["Rice, barley, and oats.", "Maize, cassava, and rice.", "Maize, wheat, and rice.", "Wheat, soybeans, and corn."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happened as a result of late blight disease?", options: ["It devastated European potato harvests during the nineteenth century.", "It only affected potato crops grown at high altitudes.", "It primarily affected potato crops across Asia.", "It was eliminated within a few years of its emergence."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what allows farmers in favourable climates to harvest potatoes multiple times a year?", options: ["The crop's short cultivation cycle.", "The use of genetically modified seeds.", "Advanced irrigation systems.", "Government subsidies for farmers."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a challenge facing potato cultivation?", options: ["Damage from the Colorado potato beetle.", "Soil salinization caused by irrigation.", "Late blight disease.", "Public scepticism toward genetically modified crops."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"subsistence\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["crop export", "comfortable living", "profitable trade", "basic living"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"scepticism\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["curiosity", "hostility", "doubt", "concern"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"these pressures\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["humid conditions", "smallholder farmers", "regulatory hurdles", "disease, pests, and climate change"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the effectiveness of genetically modified potato varieties, based on the passage?", options: ["They are primarily designed to improve the flavour of potatoes rather than their resistance to disease.", "Their success will depend not only on scientific advances but also on regulatory and public acceptance factors.", "They have already replaced traditional potato varieties in most farming regions.", "Scientists agree unanimously that genetic engineering is the only viable solution to climate-related yield loss."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Global Expansion of Disneyland: Ownership, Culture, and Adaptation", paragraphs: ["Since the opening of the original Disneyland resort in Anaheim, California, in 1955, the concept of a themed entertainment resort has expanded well beyond the United States, evolving into a network of internationally located parks that occupy a distinctive position between global corporate branding and localized cultural experience. Rather than exporting an identical format to every market, the parent company has historically relied on a range of joint-venture and licensing arrangements, an approach that has produced considerable variation in ownership structure, design philosophy, and public reception across regions.", "The first international outpost of this model, Tokyo Disneyland, opened in 1983 under a licensing arrangement with the Oriental Land Company, a Japanese firm that owns and operates the resort independently rather than through direct corporate ownership. This structure meant that the licensing company bore the financial risk of construction and operation, while paying royalties for the use of characters and design elements. The arrangement proved commercially successful largely because Japanese planners adapted certain guest-service conventions already familiar to local consumers, rather than importing an unmodified American operational template.", "A markedly different outcome accompanied the 1992 opening of Euro Disney, later rebranded Disneyland Paris. Unlike the Tokyo resort, this park was majority-owned by the parent corporation, exposing it directly to financial losses when attendance fell short of projections during its early years. Commentators at the time attributed part of the difficulty to cultural friction : some French intellectuals publicly criticised the resort as an unwelcome import of American consumer culture, while operational choices, including an initial prohibition on serving wine in park restaurants, provoked further public dissatisfaction. A substantial debt restructuring in the mid-1990s, combined with gradual adjustments to dining and pricing policies, eventually stabilised the resort's finances.", "Hong Kong Disneyland, which opened in 2005 as a joint venture between the corporation and the Hong Kong government, represents a more modest undertaking in terms of physical scale than its counterparts elsewhere. Design decisions incorporated principles of feng shui, an East Asian practice concerned with the auspicious arrangement of buildings and landscape features, reportedly influencing decisions such as the compass orientation of the main entrance and the placement of a ballroom. Despite these adaptations, the park has periodically faced criticism for insufficient scale relative to visitor demand, prompting successive rounds of expansion.", "Shanghai Disney Resort, which opened in 2016 following one of the largest single investments in the company's history, illustrates a further evolution of the localization strategy. Rather than simply translating existing attractions, designers incorporated narrative and architectural elements intended to resonate with Chinese audiences, including a garden inspired partly by classical Chinese landscape design and a distinctive castle structure that departs from the design used at other resorts. The venture operates under a joint-venture structure involving Chinese state-owned enterprises, a governance arrangement that has shaped both regulatory compliance and community engagement initiatives surrounding the resort.", "Across these resorts, several recurring adaptation strategies emerge: localized menus reflecting regional culinary preferences, calendar programming aligned with domestic holidays rather than exclusively American ones, and multilingual signage and staff training tailored to the dominant local language. Such measures reflect a broader pattern sometimes termed glocalization, whereby a globally recognisable brand retains core elements of consistency, such as character design and overall narrative themes, while permitting substantial local customisation in matters of hospitality, cuisine, and site-specific storytelling.", "Economically, these resorts have generally functioned as significant tourism drivers for their host regions, generating employment and, in several cases, prompting infrastructure investment in surrounding transport networks. Critics, however, have questioned whether the economic benefits are distributed equitably, pointing to labour practices and reliance on part-time or seasonal staffing at several locations. Whether future expansion into additional markets will replicate the joint-venture model or move toward greater corporate ownership remains uncertain, a question likely to be shaped as much by regional regulatory environments as by considerations of brand consistency."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["All Disneyland resorts located outside the United States are now more profitable than the original Anaheim park.", "International Disneyland resorts have prioritized profit maximization over cultural adaptation, with localization occurring only as a secondary consideration.", "Disneyland Paris faced serious financial difficulties primarily due to cultural misunderstandings with French audiences.", "International Disneyland resorts have expanded through a variety of ownership and licensing arrangements that balance global brand consistency with varying degrees of local cultural adaptation, producing different economic and cultural outcomes across regions."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, who owns and operates Tokyo Disneyland?", options: ["The Oriental Land Company, under a licensing arrangement.", "The parent corporation directly, without any local partner.", "A joint venture between the Japanese government and the parent corporation.", "A consortium of Japanese hospitality companies unrelated to the licensing brand."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what contributed to Disneyland Paris's financial difficulties in its early years?", options: ["A decision to relocate the resort to a different site shortly after opening.", "Cultural friction and lower-than-projected attendance, compounded by the park's direct majority ownership by the parent corporation.", "Excessive licensing fees paid to the Oriental Land Company.", "A major construction delay that postponed the opening by several years."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did feng shui principles reportedly influence the design of Hong Kong Disneyland?", options: ["By determining the total number of attractions built in the park.", "By influencing decisions such as the compass orientation of the main entrance and the placement of a ballroom.", "By requiring that all buildings be constructed using traditional materials only.", "By restricting the park's operating hours to specific times of day."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an adaptation strategy used across the international resorts?", options: ["Identical ticket pricing structures used at every international resort regardless of location.", "Calendar programming based exclusively on domestic holidays with no exceptions.", "A gradual transition toward uniform global staffing to reduce local hiring.", "Local menus reflecting regional culinary preferences."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"friction\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["enthusiasm", "hostility", "tension", "expansion"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"auspicious\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["temporary", "flawless", "favourable", "profitable"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"This structure\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the licensing arrangement with the Oriental Land Company", "Tokyo Disneyland itself", "the original Anaheim park", "corporate ownership by the parent company"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the contrast between Tokyo Disneyland and Disneyland Paris described in the passage?", options: ["Hong Kong Disneyland incorporated feng shui principles into its site design.", "Local licensing arrangements combined with early cultural adaptation may reduce a resort's financial risk exposure compared to direct majority ownership, though the passage does not confirm this pattern held across all subsequent resorts.", "Direct majority corporate ownership caused Tokyo Disneyland's early struggles, while licensing arrangements caused Disneyland Paris's success.", "Cultural friction only affected resorts owned through joint ventures, never those under direct corporate ownership."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in this passage?", options: ["To criticize the French public's reaction to Disneyland Paris.", "To praise Shanghai Disney Resort's architectural innovations above all other resorts.", "To analyse, in a largely neutral and explanatory manner, how ownership structures and cultural adaptation strategies have shaped the varying outcomes of Disneyland resorts worldwide.", "To argue that the localization strategies of international resorts have consistently failed to satisfy local audiences."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Advances in Tomato Research: Genetics, Resilience, and Trade-offs", paragraphs: ["Tomatoes rank among the most extensively studied crops in plant biology, valued not only for their agricultural and economic significance but also for their utility as a model organism for investigating fleshy fruit development, a research role that has expanded considerably since the sequencing of the cultivated tomato genome in 2012. Because this sequencing effort revealed a genome organisation shared, in broad outline, with several other economically important solanaceous crops, findings derived from tomato research often carry implications extending well beyond the crop itself.", "The 2012 genome sequencing initiative, a multinational collaborative effort, identified an estimated thirty-five thousand genes and provided researchers with a reference framework against which subsequent studies of gene function could be compared. Since that milestone, resequencing projects involving thousands of cultivated and wild tomato accessions have begun to clarify how domestication and subsequent breeding practices narrowed the crop's genetic diversity, a narrowing that some researchers argue has left modern commercial varieties more susceptible to emerging pathogens than their wild relatives.", "Breeding programmes have consequently placed considerable emphasis on reintroducing resistance traits from wild tomato relatives, particularly resistance to bacterial spot and tomato yellow leaf curl virus, two pathogens capable of causing substantial yield losses under favourable environmental conditions. Marker-assisted selection, a technique allowing breeders to track resistance genes without waiting for full plant maturation, has accelerated this process considerably, although the transfer of wild-relative traits sometimes introduces undesirable characteristics, such as reduced fruit size, that must subsequently be bred out through further crossing.", "More recently, attention has turned toward heat and drought tolerance, traits of growing importance as shifting precipitation patterns and rising average temperatures threaten yields in several major tomato-producing regions. [1] Genome-editing techniques, particularly CRISPR-based approaches, have enabled researchers to modify specific genes associated with stomatal regulation and root architecture without introducing foreign DNA, this distinction being one that some regulatory bodies treat differently from conventional genetic modification. [2] Early trial results suggest measurable improvements in water-use efficiency under controlled conditions, though field-scale validation across diverse growing environments remains limited. [3] Whether these gains will translate into commercially viable varieties depends substantially on regulatory approval processes, which vary considerably between jurisdictions. [4]", "A separate strand of research has examined the apparent decline in flavour quality associated with decades of breeding focused primarily on shelf life, uniform appearance, and transportability. Genetic studies have identified several flavour-associated compounds whose concentrations were inadvertently reduced during this selection process, findings that have since informed efforts to reintroduce these compounds without compromising the durability traits valued by commercial supply chains. Nutritionally, tomatoes remain a significant dietary source of lycopene, an antioxidant compound whose concentration, notably, tends to increase rather than decrease during cooking, a characteristic that distinguishes tomatoes from many other fruits and vegetables.", "Parallel research into controlled-environment agriculture, including hydroponic and vertical farming systems, has explored whether such methods might reduce tomato cultivation's reliance on arable land and open-field irrigation. Proponents argue that these systems allow for more precise resource management and year-round production regardless of external climate conditions. Sceptics counter that the substantial energy requirements associated with artificial lighting may offset environmental gains, particularly in regions where electricity generation still depends heavily on fossil fuels, a concern that complicates straightforward claims about the sustainability of such systems.", "Taken together, these research directions suggest that no single intervention is likely to resolve the range of challenges facing tomato production. Genetic approaches may address disease susceptibility and climate resilience but cannot, on their own, guarantee consumer acceptance of resulting varieties, particularly where public scepticism toward genetic modification persists. Similarly, controlled-environment systems may reduce certain environmental pressures while introducing others, suggesting that sustainability claims in this domain should be evaluated cautiously rather than accepted at face value.", "The cumulative trajectory of tomato research therefore reflects an argument not for any singular technological solution but for an integrated approach combining genetic innovation, cultivation-method diversification, and careful evaluation of trade-offs across environmental, economic, and consumer-acceptance dimensions. Whether this integrated approach proves sufficient to secure long-term productivity amid a changing climate remains, researchers generally concede, an open question rather than a settled conclusion."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Tomato research spans genetic, disease-resistance, climate-resilience, flavour, and cultivation-method studies, but the passage suggests that no single approach alone can resolve the crop's combined challenges, calling instead for an integrated, cautiously evaluated strategy.", "Environmental sustainability concerns surrounding controlled-environment agriculture represent the central challenge in tomato research, with genetic disease resistance serving only a minor supporting role.", "The passage argues that CRISPR gene-editing alone has already resolved the major challenges facing tomato cultivation.", "Consumer acceptance of genetically modified tomatoes has now become widespread across all major markets."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, approximately how many genes were identified during the 2012 tomato genome sequencing initiative?", options: ["Ten thousand", "Fifty thousand", "Thirty-five thousand", "Fifteen thousand"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes lycopene concentration in tomatoes from that of many other fruits and vegetables?", options: ["It only appears in tomatoes grown hydroponically.", "It was one of the compounds accidentally increased by breeding for shelf life.", "It is destroyed almost entirely during cooking.", "Its concentration tends to increase rather than decrease during cooking."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a current research direction in tomato science?", options: ["Development of standardized global regulations for genome-edited tomato varieties.", "An assumption that vertical farming will inevitably replace open-field cultivation entirely.", "Research aiming to fully eliminate tomato flavour loss without any trade-offs.", "Efforts to reintroduce disease-resistance traits from wild tomato relatives."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"susceptible\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["prone", "durable", "resistant", "exposed"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"durability\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["sturdiness", "fragility", "longevity", "resistance"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"this distinction\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the foreign DNA introduced during editing", "the regulatory bodies overseeing crops", "the difference between gene editing without foreign DNA and conventional genetic modification", "the stomatal regulation genes"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about controlled-environment agriculture, such as hydroponic or vertical farming, based on the passage?", options: ["Reduced energy requirements for artificial lighting are what allow controlled-environment systems to depend on fossil fuels.", "Controlled-environment systems allow for year-round production regardless of external climate conditions.", "Controlled-environment agriculture may reduce some environmental pressures associated with traditional cultivation, though its overall sustainability remains uncertain given potentially offsetting energy demands, a conclusion the passage stops short of fully resolving.", "Sceptics argue that controlled-environment systems eliminate all environmental concerns associated with tomato cultivation."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in this passage?", options: ["To express strong skepticism that any current tomato research direction will yield meaningful long-term benefits.", "To criticize commercial tomato breeding practices for prioritizing shelf life over flavour quality.", "To advocate specifically for CRISPR gene-editing as the most promising solution to climate-related tomato yield loss.", "To present a cautious, balanced evaluation of multiple tomato research directions, emphasizing that each approach involves trade-offs rather than offering a singular, fully validated solution."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Consequently, researchers caution against overstating the near-term commercial impact of these findings.'", options: ["[3]", "[4]", "[2]", "[1]"], answer: 0 }] },
  ] },
];
